  "review_decision": "HUMAN_TEACHING",
  "human_feedback": "Your deck planning improved this run, but you are still not treating the weaknesses identified in your own deck plan as real priorities. When the deck plan identifies a critical missing role, especially long-term damage scaling, prioritize cards that directly solve that weakness over cards that are merely efficient incremental upgrades. For example, in Act 2 you explicitly identified Strength/scaling as the deck's main missing component, but still chose Sword Boomerang over Spot Weakness. Sword Boomerang only gives more immediate attack damage, while Spot Weakness directly addresses the scaling problem you had already identified. Do not let short-term card quality override the long-term need stated in your own plan.\r\n\r\nAs the deck becomes more complete, raise the threshold for adding another card. Once damage, defense, draw and AoE are already adequately covered, a card should only beat Skip if it materially solves a named weakness, significantly strengthens an existing synergy, or enables a clear new strategy. A card being somewhat useful is not enough. If the deck plan says the deck is already large or compact and the main weakness is scaling, additions such as Armaments that do not solve that weakness should usually lose to Skip.\r\n\r\nCombat sequencing was much better this run. Continue committing to the full-turn sequence and use the cheapest sufficient defensive option. Before spending Apparition, Impervious or other limited defensive resources, calculate the actual incoming damage and the block that the planned sequence will generate. If ordinary block fully mitigates the attack, preserve the stronger limited resource; if ordinary block is insufficient, use the stronger defense rather than taking preventable damage. Base this on the actual mitigation calculation, not simply on whether multiple enemies are attacking.",
